  relatedServices={[
    { href: '/services/facebook-advertising/', label: 'Chiropractic Facebook Ads' },
    { href: '/services/web-design/', label: 'Chiropractic Web Design' },
    { href: '/services/search-engine-optimization/', label: 'SEO for Chiropractors' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>TL;DR: buy a defined workflow, not a posting promise</h2>
  <p>
    ChiroCandy offers social media content support for chiropractic practices, but the current
    proposal should be the source of truth for the platforms, content types, cadence, revisions,
    approvals, scheduling, publishing, moderation, and reporting included. The practice supplies
    accurate office facts, approved assets and permissions, and a named reviewer when the content
    depends on its people, services, locations, or offers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Organic social activity can document delivery, engagement, profile actions, and tagged visits.
    It does not, by itself, prove an inquiry, booked or held appointment, patient, or revenue result.
    Paid Facebook and Instagram campaigns are a separate service and should have their own account,
    budget, lead-handoff, and measurement scope.
  </p>

  <h2>What should a ChiroCandy social media scope define?</h2>
  <p>
    Use the table below during a proposal review. A line can be included, excluded, or owned by the
    practice or another provider, but it should not be left to assumption.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Social media service scope and ownership">
    <table>
      <caption>Scope, practice inputs, and acceptance evidence</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Workstream</th><th scope="col">Define in writing</th><th scope="col">Acceptance evidence</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Profiles and access</th><td>Named profiles, business owner, administrator access, agency permissions, and offboarding</td><td>Observed access, asset list, and a written return or removal process</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Content plan</th><td>Topics, formats, platform adaptations, planned cadence, review period, and content boundaries</td><td>Approved calendar tied to the contracted scope</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Production</th><td>Copy, graphics, captions, editing, filming, photography, revisions, and asset specifications</td><td>Approved sample and a named owner for each required input</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Approval</th><td>Practice reviewer, clinical or professional review, deadline, revision route, and missed-deadline fallback</td><td>Dated approval record for the version scheduled to publish</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Publishing</th><td>Scheduler, publishing owner, account connection, timing, quality check, and correction process</td><td>Published item on the intended profile with the approved text and media</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Community response</th><td>Comment and message monitoring, response owner, coverage hours, escalation, and prohibited topics</td><td>Tested routing and a clear handoff to the practice</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Reporting</th><td>Metrics, source systems, date window, review cadence, tagged links, and who supplies downstream outcomes</td><td>Dated report that keeps organic, paid, and practice outcomes separate</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Separate agency work from practice inputs</h2>
  <p>
    A useful engagement starts with a responsibility map. ChiroCandy can handle the agreed planning,
    copy, design, editing, scheduling, or publishing work listed in the proposal. The practice remains
    the authoritative source for its locations, hours, clinicians, services, offers, office process,
    brand preferences, and any claim that depends on its real operations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Name the person who supplies photos or footage, verifies the facts, and approves the final item.
    If a recording or approval is late, use an approved backup, move the publication date, or hold the
    item. Do not invent a doctor quote, patient story, clinical explanation, offer, or office update to
    keep a calendar full. The <a href="/blog/unlocking-chiropractic-content-ideas/">content planning guide</a>
    provides a recording brief and missed-recording fallback, while the
    <a href="/blog/chiropractic-video-content-approval-workflow/">video approval workflow</a> assigns
    script, recording, editing, and sign-off responsibilities.
  </p>

  <h2>Approve patient stories and health claims before publishing</h2>
  <p>
    A testimonial is not just another content format. The practice should verify the speaker, written
    permissions, the exact wording and media, and any result implied by the post. The
    <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/topics/truth-advertising/advertisement-endorsements">FTC's endorsement guidance</a>
    says endorsements must be truthful and not misleading and cannot make claims the advertiser could
    not substantiate directly. HHS explains that the HIPAA Privacy Rule places controls on uses or
    disclosures of protected health information for marketing; review the applicable
    <a href="https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/guidance/marketing/index.html">HHS marketing guidance</a>
    and the practice's qualified advice before using patient information.
  </p>
  <p>
    Approval should cover the visible copy, image or video, on-screen text, caption, linked page, offer,
    and scheduled account. A reviewer should be able to revise or hold an item without being treated as
    the source of a replacement clinical claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Make publishing and community response explicit</h2>
  <p>
    Content creation, scheduling, publishing, comment moderation, direct-message response, and lead
    follow-up are different jobs. The proposal should say which are included and name the owner of the
    rest. When the practice owns replies, define the notification route, coverage hours, backup, and
    topics that require private handling or escalation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not move patient details into public comments or an unapproved marketing record. A public reply
    can acknowledge the message and direct the person to an appropriate practice-controlled channel
    without discussing an individual's care. The fuller
    <a href="/blog/a-guide-to-social-media-marketing-for-chiropractors/">owner–agency social media guide</a>
    covers account control, approvals, moderation, and offboarding.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep organic social and paid advertising separate</h2>
  <p>
    Organic publishing maintains a practice-controlled profile and provides content that people may
    see or share. Paid campaigns purchase distribution under a selected objective, market, budget,
    creative, destination, and attribution setup. A post can be reused in an ad only after the paid
    scope, permissions, offer, destination, and measurement have been approved.
  </p>
  <p>
    ChiroCandy's <a href="/services/facebook-advertising/">Facebook and Instagram advertising service</a>
    defines campaign and lead-handoff questions separately. If an engagement includes both organic and
    paid work, the review should still show which fee, asset, account, result, and decision belongs to
    each workstream.
  </p>

  <h2>Use reporting to make the next content decision</h2>
  <p>
    Start with what the publishing platform can observe: delivered content, reach or impressions under
    the platform's current definitions, engagement, profile activity, and link interactions. For tagged
    links, preserve the destination, source label, and date window. These measures can help the team
    compare topics and formats; they do not establish a patient or business outcome.
  </p>
  <p>
    Record attributed inquiries in the appropriate contact system, bookings in the scheduler, held
    appointments in the practice's attendance record, and any accepted patient or financial outcome in
    its approved source. Keep unknown attribution unknown. A useful review ends with a decision—keep,
    revise, replace, or hold a content type—and the evidence behind it.
  </p>

  <h2>Confirm these items before work begins</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The exact profiles, access owner, agency permissions, and offboarding process</li>
    <li>The content types, customization level, planned cadence, and revision limit</li>
    <li>Who supplies office facts, offers, photos, video, permissions, and professional review</li>
    <li>The approver, deadline, missed-deadline fallback, and correction process</li>
    <li>Whether scheduling, publishing, comments, messages, filming, and lead response are included</li>
    <li>Which measures are reported for organic content and which belong to paid or practice systems</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Bring your current profiles, access map, recent content, approval path, and reporting questions to a
    <a href="/schedule/">Marketing &amp; AI Discovery Call</a>. ChiroCandy can compare the workflow with
    the current service scope and identify the practice inputs needed before publishing. The goal is a
    clear responsibility map and review process—not a promise about reach, inquiries, appointments,
    patients, rankings, AI citations, or revenue.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>

